In Nevada, your landlord has **30 days** after your tenancy ends and you hand back the unit. By then they have to either return your deposit or give you a written, itemized list of what they're keeping and why.

**Related rules:**
- **Penalty for missing the deadline:** If the landlord wrongly keeps your deposit, a court can order them to return all of it. It can also award you extra damages of up to the deposit amount again, so up to twice the deposit in total.
- **Deposit limit:** Everything the landlord holds as security, including any prepaid last month's rent, can't add up to more than 3 months' rent.
- **Move-in record:** A written lease has to provide for a signed record of the unit's contents and condition. If you have this, keep it. It can help if there's a dispute over damage.

**Source:** PropInspect's Nevada guide, https://propinspect.net/security-deposit-laws/nevada (last reviewed October 12, 2026), based on Nevada Revised Statutes § 118A.242 (https://www.leg.state.nv.us/NRS/NRS-118A.html#NRS118ASec242).

This is general information, not legal advice. If you end up in a dispute, you can talk to a Nevada landlord-tenant lawyer or the Nevada Supreme Court Self-Help Center (https://selfhelp.nvcourts.gov/).

